00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:46.780
So that's great.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.240
And who is a developer?

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.710
Who is using APIs?

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.040
Wow, that's 50%, maybe.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:53.630
Great.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:54.180
Nice to know.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:54.680
Great.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.060
So this might be
preaching to the choir,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.550
but I will just start
with a few slides.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:03.900
I won't spend a lot of
time on this around APIs.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.150
The guys who raised their
hand for the first question,

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:09.403
you probably are within
a digital business.

00:02:09.403 --> 00:02:11.372
[CHEERING]

00:02:11.372 --> 00:02:13.080
If they promise me at
the end of my talk,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.899
it's more louder than that.

00:02:15.899 --> 00:02:17.690
Digital businesses--
so digital businesses,

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.189
they have something
to do with APIs.

00:02:19.189 --> 00:02:23.010
They are trying to offer
services and data through APIs.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.620
And this data comes
from systems, right?

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:29.480
Backend systems, which could
be COBOL, [INAUDIBLE]----

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.150
I know banks have a
role of COBOL still.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:33.480
But it could be
brand-new systems,

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.360
like something you build on
App Engine in Google Cloud,

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.610
or a Node.js app that
you're running on your Roku,

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:39.930
for example.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:42.780
But the thing is, you want
these data and services to be

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.660
consumed by your customers,
by your partners which

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.850
you collaborate with,
and also internally

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:49.920
with an organization,
maybe for internal apps

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.180
or to your employees.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.540
So if you zoom in
to how this works,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.370
actually you come
down to this, which

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:57.840
is the digital value chain.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.620
And this is just a
slight-- we think it's

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.585
really important, with Apigee.

00:03:03.585 --> 00:03:05.460
This is what we use to
talk to our customers,

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.350
where we actually try to
investigate where you want

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.920
to go to, which of the digital
initiatives that organizations

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:14.040
want to achieve, all the way to
the other end, which backends

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.082
need to be used for that?

00:03:15.082 --> 00:03:16.998
And there's a whole bunch
in the middle, which

00:03:16.998 --> 00:03:19.110
is the developer, the guys
who raised the question

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:22.510
at the second question I asked.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.170
And there's the API team,
of course, the team that's

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:28.080
trying to operate these
APIs, trying to secure it,

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.700
trying to get these
APIs available,

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:31.869
getting them out there.

00:03:31.869 --> 00:03:34.410
And there's actually two aspects
to this digital value chain.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.860
You have the left side, which
is more on consumption, where

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.266
you actually have your APIs.

00:03:39.266 --> 00:03:40.140
They have to be used.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.265
They have to be documented.

00:03:41.265 --> 00:03:42.120
You have to get--

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.420
to find your developers, get
them in, get them onboarded.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.014
And then they can start building
and build cool new solutions.

00:03:49.014 --> 00:03:50.430
Then there's the
right part, which

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.200
is the more technical
part, where you actually

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.060
want to get data from
your backend systems

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.630
and want to make them
available, in a secure way,

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:01.110
in the right format, with
traffic management on there.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.560
We want to get analytics on the
usage and everything like that.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.550
So there are a lot of
things to think about.

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.130
So I will touch these
two aspects both,

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:11.580
but I will focus primarily
on the left side, which

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.520
is about building a
community around your API

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.890
and building a developer--

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:17.339
an API that your
developers love.

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:23.342
So there are actually
three types of APIs.

00:04:23.342 --> 00:04:25.240
There's these services,
and you're probably

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.690
all familiar with this.

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:29.860
This is APIs that
are used in SOA.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:32.290
Also in microservices,
these are APIs that

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:35.230
actually keep systems in sync.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:38.300
And typically, we want to think
about onboarding and governance

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.500
when you have API as a service.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.760
There are also APIs, more on
the left part of this triangle,

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.480
APIs as interactions, where
the purpose is actually

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:49.450
to bring these APIs out to
an app, or to a web app.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:57.400
Specifically, for making
sure that people can interact

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.420
with your organization.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:01.730
And there are
companies like Netflix,

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:05.200
for example, who are doing
this at a big scale, who

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.780
have specific APIs
for specific devices

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:11.840
and specific interactions
that they offer.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.470
And the purpose is, of course,
to make it as performance

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:19.310
and as best as possible
for the user experience.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:21.760
And this is where
techniques like GraphQL

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:23.740
come in, where the
device actually

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:28.480
asks which data it wants,
and the API actually

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.760
wants to combine several
APIs in one, just

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:33.199
to make it easy for a
device to get its data.

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:34.240
And then the last thing--

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.240
I think a sip of water.

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:43.375
The third thing is
the APIs as products.

00:05:43.375 --> 00:05:44.750
And this is,
typically, where you

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:48.260
see that organization
who are not in technology

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:49.580
see something new.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:51.680
Apart from offering
physical products,

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:54.500
your APIs, your
interactions, can also

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:57.560
be a product that you
offer to your customers

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.890
or to your partners,
for example.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:02.257
And you could also think
about monetizing this,

00:06:02.257 --> 00:06:03.590
asking money for these projects.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:05.420
You are offering
something, so you

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:09.590
could think about a monetization
strategy on top of your APIs.

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:12.830
So that having said, these
APIs fit in ecosystems.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.710
And if you think about the
Facebook API, the Google Maps

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:20.230
API, or the Twitter API, these
are all public ecosystems

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:21.620
that everybody knows.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:24.080
But it turns out that
most APIs are actually

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:26.270
internal-- internal to
organizations, where they're

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:29.870
being used within the
organization for apps,

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:32.860
or maybe just for apps
that the company has

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:34.705
out there in the market.

00:06:34.705 --> 00:06:36.080
And there's
something interesting

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:37.670
happening in the
middle layers, where

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:40.940
we used to have organizations
working together

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:43.490
with their partners as a
business-to-business model,

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:45.950
where they used to offer
technologies like Swift,

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:50.390
or FTPing files to each
other, or maybe sending tapes

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:51.370
to each other.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:53.170
And we see that these
interactions are now

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:56.030
turning to become more and
more going over APIs as well.

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:03.710
So if you think about this,
most APIs are internal,

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:05.460
and the minority of
APIs are actually

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:09.810
these APIs that have these
beautiful developer portals.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:12.930
Why should we care about what
a developer thinks of these?

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.330
So we sometimes
talk to customers,

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:17.460
and they say, well, we
are offering a service.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:21.060
We are a big bank, or
we are a prime retailer.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:23.205
And the developers, they
just have to use our API

00:07:23.205 --> 00:07:25.090
and they have no choice.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:28.030
And yeah, you could
be right with that.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.690
But if you think about
it a little bit more,

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:32.760
there's a lot of friction
that you are actually

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:34.280
adding to every
interaction that you

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:39.540
are doing, if your API is not
an API that developers love.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:43.280
And just that these developers,
they don't want to waste time.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:45.820
It's not that they're lazy, but
they want to do things easy.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:49.270
If they can use an SDK and
just have a few lines of code

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:52.500
to interact with your
company, then they're happy.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.092
And if they're happy,
there's a chance

00:07:54.092 --> 00:07:56.550
that they're using your APIs
more and more, with more great

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.260
ideas, and more interactions.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:07:59.635
And that's what
it actually out--

00:07:59.635 --> 00:08:00.634
that's what [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:00.634 --> 00:08:03.360
We're not changing the business
model for our customers.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.980
We're making sure that they
can do the thing they're doing.

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:07.980
If it's a retailer and
they're selling products,

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:10.105
it's just that we're selling
products through APIs.

00:08:10.105 --> 00:08:11.970
So the more
interactions you have,

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:13.650
the better the
organization works.

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:16.320
And that's why it's
important that developers

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.710
care about your API.

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:22.260
So that's my introduction.

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:25.590
I just want to go over
a few things which

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.480
we've seen within organizations
that actually make a great API.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.650
And it's just my
list of seven things.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:33.630
And we'll just get
over them one by one.

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:35.539
And after that, I'll
move into a demo,

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:38.409
where I'm actually going to
show what's on the slide here.

00:08:38.409 --> 00:08:42.032
So things like self-service,
like great documentation,

00:08:42.032 --> 00:08:44.490
or a low-friction sign-up, they
are tremendously important.

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:47.430
And the most popular API
programs out there, they

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:48.260
do exactly this.

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:55.110
So first, these are screenshots
of customers of Apigee,

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:58.240
with their developer portal.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.850
The thing with
these companies is

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.190
that they have a really
easy developer portal,

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:04.350
where it's quick to
discover what the APIs are,

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:07.710
what interactions developers can
have with these organizations.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:09.870
And it's really easy
to go push a button

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:11.640
and get access immediately.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:14.640
Maybe it's not full API, or
maybe it's just mock data,

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:17.820
or maybe there's just
a rate limit on it.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:19.080
But it's easy to get access.

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:21.420
It's easy to go into
the developer portal

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:22.420
and to get started.

00:09:25.577 --> 00:09:27.660
The other thing, which is
closely related to this,

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:30.220
is if you want to get
started, you need onboard.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:33.210
You need to make yourself
known to the organization.

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:34.914
And if you have to
know the guy, if you

00:09:34.914 --> 00:09:37.080
have to attend the meeting,
if they have to type you

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:40.450
into a database, and then
it takes three weeks,

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:41.940
then there's a lot of friction.

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:44.230
And people, or developers,
they will easily

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:46.710
turn from one API to
another if they can get

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:49.190
started quickly,
because developers,

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:51.760
they want to do things quickly.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:53.610
So this is a screenshot
of the developer

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:55.620
portal of Ticketmaster.

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:57.670
Probably you're aware
of, they sell tickets.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:59.670
But 10% of their
ticket sales is now

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:02.455
going through APIs, which they
are not selling themselves,

00:10:02.455 --> 00:10:06.060
but they are sold through
third-party apps or web apps.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:08.940
And I like to think that
this is just because it's

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:10.020
so easy to sign up.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:13.270
It's so easy to find yourself
their APIs to get started with,

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:15.210
is that they are finding
a lot of developers.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:17.130
There's not a whole
lot of fields on there

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:18.166
to be filled in.

00:10:18.166 --> 00:10:20.040
There's no social security
number or anything

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:21.520
that you need to think about.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:24.390
Of course, you need to accept
the terms of conditions, terms

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:27.160
and conditions, but
nobody reads them anyway.

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:28.590
You can just scroll through it.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:31.470
I didn't say that.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:33.570
And then this is
about documentation.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:36.480
Developers, they like to
know how your API works.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:38.744
So there should be a place
where you could actually

00:10:38.744 --> 00:10:41.160
scroll through all the APIs,
have an overview of what they

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.540
do, and then get some more
details on which headers

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:45.529
you need to use,
which URLs to call,

00:10:45.529 --> 00:10:47.820
which parameters I can provide,
and anything like that.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:49.320
And there are great
tools out there

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:51.360
these days, like OpenAPI--

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:52.980
it used to be called Swagger--

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:55.950
which can help you automatically
generate, from an API design,

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:57.960
great and beautiful
documentation.

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:01.610
And that's what you want to
offer to your developers.

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:04.060
But it's not just
documentation, right?

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:06.090
You could have really
beautiful documentation,

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:07.980
but if your API
design sucks, it's

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:10.210
not going to be successful.

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:14.100
So developers actually
know what they don't like.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:18.390
If you look on the slide,
it's like, get all docs,

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:21.916
and save doc, and that's
quite easy, still,

00:11:21.916 --> 00:11:23.040
to understand what it does.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:26.580
But if you think about
create recurring doc walk,

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:29.940
or replace sitting
docs with running docs,

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:32.020
it's getting to
start complicated.

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:33.520
Then that's actually
if you-- if you

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:34.924
follow such kind
of model, that's

00:11:34.924 --> 00:11:36.090
actually where it turns out.

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:38.670
And developers, they have
to research for a long time.

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:40.800
They have to read all the
documentation to actually

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.690
understand what your API does.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:44.820
So don't do this.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:48.430
And do something like this.

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:49.920
So the developers
in the audience

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:53.390
have asked you about REST, and
if I tell you my API is REST,

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:56.340
you probably have a pretty
good idea how the API works.

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:58.890
You won't need a lot of
study, a lot of research,

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:00.030
to actually use my API.

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:03.180
It's just a bunch
of URLs, which are

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:06.960
entities, which includes get
to read from those entities

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:09.140
to get a list, or search.

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:10.724
And you could create entities.

00:12:10.724 --> 00:12:11.640
You could update them.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:12.556
You could delete them.

00:12:12.556 --> 00:12:16.230
And it's quite easy
how everything works.

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:19.080
And if you're building
some weird own protocol

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:22.729
or something, it's not going
to make any developer happy.

00:12:22.729 --> 00:12:24.270
So there are a bunch
of other things,

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:27.390
like the RPC GraphQ
on Hypermedia,

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:30.810
which are kind of new
things, new tools that you

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:32.510
can offer to your developers.

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:35.820
RPC is, and primarily
Google is investing in it

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:38.850
with gRPC, which is a protocol
that you could use when

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:40.770
performance is really
important and you

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:43.050
have a pretty good idea
how your API works,

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:45.220
and it's not going
to change a lot.

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:47.550
And things like GraphQL,
I mentioned it earlier,

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:50.640
where the device actually asks
for exactly what data it needs,

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:51.750
like a query.

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:55.120
And the API makes sure that
exactly that data is sent back.

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:57.480
So it saves the device
probably making a few calls

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.520
to get the data it needs.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.210
And then there's Hypermedia,
which is actually an API.

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:05.880
In every response, there
is a URL, or multiple URLs.

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:07.610
So developers actually
read the response

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:10.680
and follow the URLs in there
to go from one API call

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:15.510
to another, and actually follow
the lead, follow the links.

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:18.930
So when you're using this, and
use it with sense, of course,

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:22.260
you could actually build an API
that's well-known by developers

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:24.070
because these are
well-known techniques,

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:25.805
but also really
fully functional.

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:31.830
The same thing with API
design holds for security.

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:35.020
If you stick to the things
that your developers know--

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:37.720
so if you're building your
own SDK, you compile it.

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:38.862
You give it to developers.

00:13:38.862 --> 00:13:40.570
And you say to the
developers, well, this

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:42.590
is what you have to
use to use our API.

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:43.990
This SDK does the security.

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:46.030
But we're not going to
tell you what it does,

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:48.610
and we're not going to provide
you the sourcecode anywhere.

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:51.220
The developer is
basically in the dark.

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:54.610
And I'm wondering if they
even trust this, right?

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:56.470
So what you need
to do is use things

00:13:56.470 --> 00:13:59.320
like OAuth, which has become
a kind of a de facto standard

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:02.420
for security when you need
to do user authentication.

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:06.130
This user can change his
user credentials for a token,

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:10.000
and then with the token, they
can do some actions on the API,

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:12.040
specifically for one
and only one user

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:15.080
from one and only one device.

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:20.064
But our APIs, for example, a get
location or get docs location,

00:14:20.064 --> 00:14:21.730
I don't know, an API
that doesn't really

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:23.860
need user authentication,
for those APIs,

00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:26.230
it might be fine just
to have an API key.

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:28.420
Right, it's not that
you need to use the most

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:29.720
security for all your APIs.

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:33.280
Make it easy where you can, and
just send a client ID or an API

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:36.040
key for an API where
that's sufficient.

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:41.930
And if you do all that,
without going any further,

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:43.450
I think you're
doing a great job.

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:46.250
And I think your APIs
will be really great,

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:49.300
and developers will be
enthusiastic about it.

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:52.750
But you could take it a little
further, and most popular APIs

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:55.915
from companies out there
like Google Cloud or Twitter,

00:14:55.915 --> 00:14:59.440
they have these
SDKs, which just make

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:01.180
it a little bit
easier for developers

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:03.730
to start using your
APIs from their code.

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:05.380
Of course, you
have to offer SDKs

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:08.740
for the most popular
programming platforms,

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:11.260
like Go, Java, Node.js, Python.

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:13.420
And of course, not
only offer the API,

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:15.400
but also provide
sourcecode of the API.

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:16.900
So that's why you
see here there are

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:18.191
links to get them, for example.

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:24.150
And then there is
another thing, which

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:26.929
