00:23:17.912 --> 00:23:18.870
So this is Apigee Edge.

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:22.880
I'm now logged in more to the
UI where the API team works.

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:24.830
So in this UI, I
can manage my APIs,

00:23:24.830 --> 00:23:27.800
and they are called API proxies,
because they are actually

00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:29.060
an API proxy that--

00:23:29.060 --> 00:23:32.090
proxy for something
in the backend.

00:23:32.090 --> 00:23:35.960
So my API that I want to show
you is called The Caravan

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:39.080
Tracker, and for those
of you who are not Dutch,

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:42.440
you probably know,
understand, that the Dutch,

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:45.290
they go on holiday during the
summer with their caravans.

00:23:45.290 --> 00:23:47.780
And the biggest problem for
the Dutch guys in the audience

00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:50.390
is to figure out how
heavy their caravan can

00:23:50.390 --> 00:23:51.920
be behind their car, right?

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:53.600
They know their car.

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:55.910
The big question is, how
heavy can my caravan be?

00:23:55.910 --> 00:23:58.040
What's the maximum
weight I can tow?

00:23:58.040 --> 00:24:00.050
So this API is actually
helping with that.

00:24:00.050 --> 00:24:01.209
And it's just an example.

00:24:01.209 --> 00:24:02.750
But in this example,
I'd like to show

00:24:02.750 --> 00:24:06.500
what Apigee is adding
from functionality on

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:08.990
to make this API.

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:10.910
So this is the overview
page of my API.

00:24:10.910 --> 00:24:12.451
And the thing that's
really important

00:24:12.451 --> 00:24:16.160
is how I can call it, the
URL, and the backend, which

00:24:16.160 --> 00:24:18.840
is the backend that lives
somewhere in the organization.

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:22.565
In this case, it's a backend
from the RDW, the [INAUDIBLE],,

00:24:22.565 --> 00:24:24.680
the government agency
that keeps track

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:27.962
of all the cars on the
roads in the Netherlands.

00:24:27.962 --> 00:24:29.420
They've published
a data set that I

00:24:29.420 --> 00:24:33.110
can query with a license plate,
with a Dutch license plate.

00:24:33.110 --> 00:24:34.830
So that's what I've done.

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:37.910
I want to quickly
open up my trace.

00:24:37.910 --> 00:24:40.130
And on here, I can
visualize all the API

00:24:40.130 --> 00:24:42.067
calls that are being executed.

00:24:42.067 --> 00:24:44.150
All the API calls that
come in will be visualized.

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:46.885
And I can quickly explain you
what Apigee is doing in there.

00:24:46.885 --> 00:24:49.010
So what I'm going to do,
I'm going to open my app--

00:24:49.010 --> 00:24:50.880
hopefully I can visualize
it on the screen--

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:53.852
make a few calls, and then
explain what's happening.

00:25:02.230 --> 00:25:06.530
So let me get it both on one
screen, if that's possible.

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:07.510
No, it's not.

00:25:07.510 --> 00:25:08.210
Ah, it is.

00:25:12.660 --> 00:25:15.080
So the trace is running
in the background,

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:16.240
and I have my app here.

00:25:16.240 --> 00:25:19.160
I pre-filled the
license plate of my car,

00:25:19.160 --> 00:25:21.330
and I pre-filled the weight
of a certain caravan.

00:25:21.330 --> 00:25:25.490
Let's say, let's assume
my caravan is 2,000 kilos.

00:25:25.490 --> 00:25:30.380
So I make a call, and we get
back information about my car,

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:32.760
about the weight of my car,
the weight of the caravan I

00:25:32.760 --> 00:25:34.880
had requested, and
the maximum advised.

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:37.310
So my Porsche 911--

00:25:37.310 --> 00:25:38.720
it's not really my car.

00:25:38.720 --> 00:25:40.810
But it cannot tow anything.

00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:43.470
It's typical for these sports
car, so they're useless.

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:46.040
They are useless
to go on holiday.

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:48.272
So that's why the
caravan is too heavy.

00:25:48.272 --> 00:25:49.730
But really, it's
not about the app.

00:25:49.730 --> 00:25:52.820
It's just a funny example
to make an API call.

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:54.860
In the background, you
actually see the API.

00:25:54.860 --> 00:25:57.620
And you see that there is some
icons popping up and all that,

00:25:57.620 --> 00:25:58.760
which I will explain.

00:25:58.760 --> 00:26:03.650
I will just make another call,
and quickly make another call.

00:26:03.650 --> 00:26:04.760
And there's a spike.

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:06.290
Yes.

00:26:06.290 --> 00:26:07.820
So there were three calls.

00:26:07.820 --> 00:26:09.230
Third one will hopefully pop up.

00:26:09.230 --> 00:26:10.740
There it is.

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:14.240
And we see a kind of a flow
from the app to the backend,

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:16.740
and then the response from
the backend back to the app.

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:19.100
So that's actually what an
API proxy is doing in Apigee.

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:20.917
It's kind of in the middle.

00:26:20.917 --> 00:26:23.000
And it can do a lot of
things being in the middle.

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:24.320
It can add security.

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:26.260
So we see the little
padlock in the icon,

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:28.400
and we call these
icons "policies."

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:30.470
That is adding an
API key verification.

00:26:30.470 --> 00:26:32.540
So it requires
the app to provide

00:26:32.540 --> 00:26:34.580
an API key, one of
the security things I

00:26:34.580 --> 00:26:36.050
had on my slides, right?

00:26:36.050 --> 00:26:38.540
We're also protecting against
too much traffic, which

00:26:38.540 --> 00:26:41.737
means that somebody is
maybe misusing the API,

00:26:41.737 --> 00:26:43.570
or maybe somebody is
really too enthusiastic

00:26:43.570 --> 00:26:46.130
and he wants to test
all the cars he sees.

00:26:46.130 --> 00:26:49.610
But typically, the spike
arrest policy will block this.

00:26:49.610 --> 00:26:51.807
And they will prevent
too much traffic.

00:26:51.807 --> 00:26:54.140
And too much traffic is not
really a problem for Apigee,

00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:56.250
but it sometimes
is for the backend.

00:26:56.250 --> 00:26:58.710
So that's why we
want to stop it.

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:00.960
So other things we are doing
is, for example, caching.

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:03.260
So we see my second call,
it was a lot quicker,

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:07.040
from 421 milliseconds to 53.

00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:08.030
So that's great.

00:27:08.030 --> 00:27:09.680
53 milliseconds for an API call.

00:27:09.680 --> 00:27:12.499
And that's because we don't
need to get to the RDW

00:27:12.499 --> 00:27:13.790
to get the data about this car.

00:27:13.790 --> 00:27:14.630
We already know it.

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:15.510
We have cached this.

00:27:15.510 --> 00:27:17.904
So providing a good caching
strategy, of course,

00:27:17.904 --> 00:27:19.820
is something that you
want to put in your API.

00:27:22.414 --> 00:27:23.830
Other things I'm
doing, I won't go

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:26.410
into a lot of technical details,
but the backend actually

00:27:26.410 --> 00:27:28.810
returns all the data
I know about this car.

00:27:28.810 --> 00:27:32.020
So they will have the price, the
catalog price of this Porsche.

00:27:32.020 --> 00:27:33.370
They'll have the weight of
this Porsche, of course.

00:27:33.370 --> 00:27:34.030
That's what I need.

00:27:34.030 --> 00:27:35.110
They have the color
of the Porsche.

00:27:35.110 --> 00:27:36.360
That's something I don't need.

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:38.260
So I actually can filter
out exactly what's

00:27:38.260 --> 00:27:41.380
required for my API and
send back the right data

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:43.920
to be shown and
be used in my app.

00:27:43.920 --> 00:27:47.320
So this is just a quick
example on what Apigee can do.

00:27:47.320 --> 00:27:50.470
On the technical part of
the digital value chain,

00:27:50.470 --> 00:27:53.500
making your APIs available
with the right security,

00:27:53.500 --> 00:27:55.780
the right data, and the
right technical formats.

00:27:55.780 --> 00:27:57.850
So just to conclude,
what you have seen

00:27:57.850 --> 00:27:59.470
is how you could build your API.

00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:03.037
You've seen loads of examples
of developer portals, of APIs.

00:28:03.037 --> 00:28:05.120
How this should look like,
what you should not do,

00:28:05.120 --> 00:28:07.160
and what you should do.

00:28:07.160 --> 00:28:09.520
And I hope you
found it interesting

00:28:09.520 --> 00:28:12.730
and you have a lot of fun
using or exposing your APIs.

00:28:12.730 --> 00:28:15.250
We will here, waiting for
you, if you have questions

00:28:15.250 --> 00:28:18.020
or you want to talk with
us about ABN AMRO case,

00:28:18.020 --> 00:28:18.970
or about Apigee.

00:28:18.970 --> 00:28:23.449
And we like to see you
here, if that's the case.

00:28:23.449 --> 00:28:25.240
Thank you for coming,
and have a great day.

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:28.290
[APPLAUSE]

